¶
'''whip-hanger''' (hwip′hang″ėr), ''n.'' A device for holding carriage-whips in a harness-room; a whip-rack.

'''whip-hem''' (hwip′hem), ''n.'' A hem formed by whipping an edge, as of a ruffle, etc. See ''whip'', ''v. t.'', 4.
{{smaller block|Bits of ruffling peeping out from the folds, with their edges in almost invisible ''whip-hems.''
{{right|''Mrs. Whitney'', Leslie Goldthwaite, i.}}}}

'''whipjack''' (hwip′jak), ''n.'' A vagabond who begs for alms as a distressed seaman: hence a general term of reproach or contempt.
{{smaller block|A mere ''whip-jack'', and that is, in the commonwealth of rogues, a slave that can talk of sea-fight, . . . yet indeed all his service is by land, and that is to rob a fair, or some such venturous exploit.
{{right|''Middleton and Dekker'', Roaring Girl, v. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|Albeit one Boner (a bare ''whippe Jacke'') for lucre of money toke vpon him to be thy father, and than to mary thy mother, yet thou wast persone Savage’s bastarde.
{{right|''Bp. Ponet'' (Maitland on Reformation, p. 74). (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''whip-king†''' (hwip′king), ''n.'' [< ''whip'', ''v.'', + obj. ''king''<sup>1</sup>.] A ruler of kings; a king-maker.
{{smaller block|Richard Nevill, that ''whip-king'' (as some tearmed him), . . . going about . . . to turn and translate scepters at his pleasure. ''Holland'', tr. of Camden, p. 571. (''Davies.'')}}

