'''whippet''' (hwip′et), ''n.'' [Cf. ''whiffet.''] A kind of dog, in breed between a greyhound and a spaniel. ''Halliwell.''
{{smaller block|In the shapes and formes of dogges; of all which there are but two sorts that are usefull for mans profit, which two are the mastiffe, and the little curre, ''whippet'', or house-dogge; all the rest are for pleasure and recreation.
{{right|''John Taylor'', Works. (''Nares.'')}}}}

'''whippincrust†''', ''n.'' A variety of wine (?).
{{smaller block|I’ll give thee white wine, red wine, claret wine, sack, muskadine, malmsey, and ''whippincrust.''
{{right|''Marlowe'', Faustus, ii. 3.}}}}

'''whipping''' (hwip′ing), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''whip'', ''v.''] '''1.''' A beating; flagellation.
{{smaller block|Use every man after his desert, and who should ’scape ''whipping?''
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, ii. 2. 556.}}
No nuns, no monks, no fakeers, take ''whippings'' more kindly than some devotees of the world.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Philip, iv.}}}}
