'''whippletree''' (hwip′l-trē), ''n.'' Same as ''whiffletree.''

'''whippoorwill''' (hwip′pọ̈r-wil′), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''whippowill'' (cf. ''poor-will''); an imitative word, from the sound or cry made by the bird, as if ‘whip poor Will.’] An American caprimulgine bird, ''Antrostomus vociferus'', related to the chuck-will’s-widow, ''A. carolinensis'', and resembling the European goatsucker, ''Caprimulgus europæus.'' {{smaller|It is 9 to 10 inches long, and 16 to 18 in extent of wings (being thus much smaller than the chuck-}}
